---
import JournalLayout from '../layouts/JournalLayout.astro';
---
<JournalLayout
  title="For Public Schools — The Journal"
  description="Bronze, Silver, and Gold canisters — sovereign compute units for students, schools, and districts. Built for public schools. Free for evaluation."
  eyebrow="§ V — For Public Schools"
>
  <header class="measure stack" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
    <h1>For Public Schools</h1>
    <p class="muted" style="font-size: var(--t-h5); line-height: 1.5;">
      This work was built to be useful to public schools.
    </p>
  </header>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="measure stack-lg">
    <article class="stack">
      <span class="eyebrow">For a student</span>
      <h2 class="latin">Bronze Canister</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>Bronze Canister</strong> is a sovereign compute unit for a single
        student — their own AI tutor, their own memory vault, their own voice
        interface. The student owns it. The school does not. The district does not.
        We do not.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Voice-native. Persistent. The student&apos;s memories, the student&apos;s notes,
        the student&apos;s learning trajectory — encrypted with substrate-level keys
        only the student controls.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="stack">
      <span class="eyebrow">For a school</span>
      <h2 class="latin">Silver Canister</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>Silver Canister</strong> orchestrates many Bronze Canisters at the
        school level — without ever reading a student&apos;s private memory.
        It tracks what a school has to track (attendance, scheduling, curriculum,
        announcements, the academic calendar) and nothing else.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Sovereignty-safe by construction: the metadata it reads is metadata,
        not content. A teacher dashboard sees the activity surface; the
        underlying memory is the student&apos;s.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="stack">
      <span class="eyebrow">For a district</span>
      <h2 class="latin">Gold Canister</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>Gold Canister</strong> sits at the district level. It does
        the work a district has to do — compliance, state reporting,
        graduation tracking, audit — and it protects the schools beneath it
        from data exposure they cannot afford to carry alone.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        FERPA-respectful by default. The compliance engine runs against the
        metadata, not the content. The content stays where it belongs.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="measure stack">
    <span class="eyebrow">The laws that govern how this speaks to a child</span>
    <h2>LEGES ANIMAE · Behavioural Communication Laws</h2>
    <p>
      Eight laws — L72 through L79 — govern how this system communicates with
      students, parents, and staff. They are encoded in
      <a href="/papers/v-behavioral-economics-laws">Paper V — <em class="latin">Leges Animae</em></a>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>L72</strong> Anchoring</td>             <td>Every recommendation is framed relative to the student&apos;s current position.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>L73</strong> Loss weight Λ = 2.25</td>  <td>Losses are felt 2.25× more than equivalent gains. The system corrects for this; it does not exploit it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>L74</strong> Cost of staying still</td> <td>The status quo is always priced. No free inaction.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>L75</strong> Endowment correction</td>  <td>Things already owned (a study habit, a method) are weighted appropriately in change analysis.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>L76</strong> Time language</td>         <td>Future value is expressed in the student&apos;s actual decision horizon, not in adult-sized units.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>L77</strong> Probability shape</td>     <td>Human probability perception (over-weighting small chances, under-weighting moderate ones) is corrected for.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>L78</strong> The right to both frames</td><td>Gain and loss framing are always offered. The system never picks the frame to influence the decision.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>L79</strong> Regret minimisation</td>   <td>For irreversible decisions, a regret analysis is shown alongside expected value.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="measure stack">
    <h2>Why this</h2>
    <p>
      We built this for public schools, for students, for the kids who do not
      get to choose their AI. We are not selling it. We are talking to the
      districts who want it directly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The architecture sits in three public papers anyone can read now:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/papers/v-behavioral-economics-laws">Paper V — <em class="latin">Leges Animae</em></a> · the behavioural communication laws.</li>
      <li><a href="/papers/xviii-archivum-memoriae">Paper XVIII — <em class="latin">Archivum Memoriae</em></a> · sovereign memory vaults.</li>
      <li><a href="/papers/xix-infrastructura-civica">Paper XIX — <em class="latin">Infrastructura Civica</em></a> · civic infrastructure as sovereign intelligence.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you work in or with a public school system and want to talk:
      <a href="mailto:Medinasitech@outlook.com?subject=Public%20School%20Inquiry"><strong>Medinasitech@outlook.com</strong></a>.
      Subject line:&nbsp;<em>Public School Inquiry</em>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <aside class="doctrine">
    <h6>What we are not doing on this page</h6>
    <p>
      We do not name a district. We do not name a school. We do not name a
      student. The relationships we have with public school systems stay
      between the people doing the work and the people receiving it.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the give.
    </p>
  </aside>
</JournalLayout>
